description:
The normalized key under which an accepted direct message is remembered for the bounded TTL. Every field is here because leaving it out breaks something specific:

- `source_peer` — authenticated, so one peer cannot suppress another's delivery;
- `source_endpoint` — without it, two endpoints on the SAME authenticated peer reusing a message ID would collapse into one delivery, and the second message would silently vanish;
- `destination_selector` — the sender's addressing, not the resolved endpoint, so a default that changes mid-TTL cannot turn one logical message into two;
- `message_id` — the sender's idempotency key, reused deliberately on retry.

The stored VALUE (first resolved endpoint plus DirectContentFingerprintV1) is not part of the key. Same key with a different fingerprint is a duplicate-ID conflict, rejected rather than delivered — one idempotency key must not alias two different bodies.

This shape is local state, not wire bytes; it is defined here so implementations agree on what identity means for a retry.
